# --- build output (regenerated by dotnet build/publish) ---
GK/source/bin/
GK/source/obj/
GK/source/publish/
GK/rules/bin/
GK/rules/obj/
GK/smoke/bin/
GK/smoke/obj/
# Never commit the published exe. Dropping ReadyToRun and single-file compression in
# v1.11.0 (a 3.5x faster first launch — see the csproj) took it to ~155 MB, past
# GitHub's 100 MB per-file limit; an autosync run that swept it in made the push fail
# with an HTTP 408 that reads like a network problem and is not one. It ships the way
# GritKeeper/app/ already does: as an asset on the GitHub Release.
GK/publish/

# --- packaged deliverables (regenerated from GK/source; app/ is ~160 MB of
#     self-contained .NET runtime, the zip ~70 MB — both rebuilt on release) ---
GritKeeper/app/
GritKeeper.zip
# GritKeeper/source/ was a byte-identical second copy of GK/source tracked in git for no
# build reason — and the exact seam that silently diverged once (2026-07-10). It is now a
# generated mirror like app/: overwritten from GK/source as a step of packaging, never
# hand-edited, never carried between sessions in git. GK/source is the only source of truth.
GritKeeper/source/
# Same story for the rules library, mirrored beside source/ since v1.28.0 (the app's
# ProjectReference points at ..\rules\, so the two must ship as siblings).
GritKeeper/rules/
# And the license pair, copied in by package.ps1 from the repo root at v1.29.1 so the ZIP carries
# them. Copies, not sources — tracking them would put a second LICENSE in the repo that has to be
# kept in step with the first, which is the same duplicate-tracked-copy mistake GritKeeper/source/
# was. The root LICENSE and NOTICE are the only real ones.
GritKeeper/LICENSE
GritKeeper/NOTICE
# Per-release notes are a release asset like the zip: written to be pasted into the GitHub
# Release, and redundant the moment it is published — the text then lives on the Release and
# the history lives in CHANGELOG.md. Seven of them (v1.16.2–v1.20.1) accumulated at the repo
# root before this rule; they were removed on 2026-07-27, and are still recoverable from git
# history and from the Releases themselves. Write the next one, paste it, let it stay local.
RELEASE_NOTES_v*.md

# --- per-table runtime state (the Keeper's live session, not project source) ---
session.json
startup-error.txt

# --- autosync noise ---
autosync.log

# --- local-only: what GitHub carries is the three books, GritKeeper, and what supports them ---
# Standing rule from 2026-07-29 (user's): the public repo holds the deliverables and the files
# that BUILD or CHECK them — nothing else. These six stay on this disk, still doing their jobs;
# they were untracked with `git rm --cached`, so they are recoverable from history if wanted.
#   * the machine's own setup — a scheduled backup task and a Claude Code session command; they
#     build nothing and describe this laptop, not the game. (Note the cross-repo convention in
#     CLAUDE.md: autosync.ps1 is meant to be identical in every Desktop\Git repo. It still is on
#     disk — it is simply no longer PUBLISHED from this one.)
autosync.ps1
register_autosync_task.ps1
.claude/
#   * process records: an unbuilt design proposal and a log of editorial findings that were
#     declined. Both are conversation, not product. The online-play proposal's substance — the
#     four rungs and the two credential/RNG gotchas — is summarized in CLAUDE.md's roadmap so
#     nothing is lost by the file being local.
DESIGN-online-play.md
ONLINE-PLAY-BRIEF.md
editorial-denials.md
#   * a dead one-shot: add_index.py baked the v2.9 Index into a source file that no longer exists
#     (player-src.html, retired 2026-07-18) and has been marked "do not re-run" ever since.
add_index.py

# --- python / editor debris ---
__pycache__/
*.pyc
_map_preview.html

# Runtime files package.ps1 moves out of GritKeeper\app before zipping. Never shipped,
# never committed — they are whatever the machine that ran the app happened to leave behind.
.package-aside/
